Prime numbers are fundamental to number theory and computational mathematics. A prime number is a natural number greater than one that has exactly two divisors: one and itself. Seven, for instance, qualifies as prime because only one and seven divide it evenly with no remainder. Testing whether a number is prime requires checking its divisibility against potential factors. Here is a Scala function that performs this check efficiently: 
```scala
def isPrime(n: Int): Boolean = {
  if (n <= 1) false
  else if (n == 2) true
  else if (n % 2 == 0) false
  else {
    for (i <- 3 to Math.sqrt(n).toInt by 2) {
      if (n % i == 0) return false
    }
    true
  }
}
```
 This function returns true when given a prime number and false otherwise. The algorithm optimizes by checking only odd divisors up to the square root of the input, since any factor larger than the square root must have a corresponding factor smaller than it. By applying this function systematically across a range of integers, mathematicians and programmers can identify all primes within that range and study their properties and distributions.

