The water cycle continuously circulates moisture through Earth's atmosphere in a process that scientists model using computational systems. Clouds form when water vapor rises, cools, and condenses around microscopic particles. Once clouds accumulate sufficient water droplets, precipitation occurs and rain falls from clouds toward the ground below. Consider how a weather simulation might track this downward movement: 
