Water exists in three physical states: solid, liquid, and gas. When water freezes, its molecules slow down and arrange themselves into a rigid crystalline structure, forming ice. This transformation occurs at 0 degrees Celsius under standard atmospheric pressure. Understanding phase transitions helps us model temperature changes in scientific applications. Consider a simple Java program that tracks the state of water as temperature decreases: 
```java
public class WaterPhase {
    public static String determinePhase(double temperatureCelsius) {
        if (temperatureCelsius > 0) {
            return "liquid";
        } else if (temperatureCelsius == 0) {
            return "transitioning";
        } else {
            return "solid (ice)";
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (double temp = 10; temp >= -10; temp -= 5) {
            System.out.println("At " + temp + "C: " + determinePhase(temp));
        }
    }
}
```
 This program demonstrates how water's physical state changes as temperature drops. Ice, which is frozen water, forms when the temperature falls below the freezing point. The rigid structure of ice makes it less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats on water's surface.
